            ("Newburgh and Continentalist essays", "After Yorktown he represented New York in the Confederation Congress and wrote Continentalist essays calling for stronger national taxing power. During the Newburgh crisis, he communicated with dissatisfied officers while Washington ultimately defused the confrontation."),
            ("Eliza Schuyler", "His marriage joined him to Philip Schuyler's powerful New York family. Eliza later preserved his papers and helped establish the Orphan Asylum Society."),
            ("Seabury pamphlet battle", "He answered loyalist Samuel Seabury's Letters of a Westchester Farmer with A Full Vindication and The Farmer Refuted. These youthful writings opened IAC's 2018 playoff question."),
            ("Annapolis Convention", "He attended the poorly subscribed 1786 meeting on interstate commerce and helped James Madison draft its call for a broader convention at Philadelphia. That recommendation became the immediate institutional bridge to the Constitutional Convention."),
            ("Constitution and Publius", "At Philadelphia he advocated an extremely strong executive and Senate. He then wrote most of the Federalist essays alongside James Madison and John Jay."),
            ("Federalist authorship map", "He wrote fifty-one of the eighty-five Federalist essays; Madison wrote twenty-nine and Jay five. Hamilton handled many institutional and executive topics, including the energetic executive in Federalist No. 70."),
            ("New York ratification", "At the Poughkeepsie convention, he debated Anti-Federalists led by Governor George Clinton and Melancton Smith. New York ratified narrowly after recommending amendments and reserving confidence in the new union."),
            ("Public credit and assumption", "As treasury secretary he proposed funding federal securities at face value and assuming state Revolutionary debts. The measures tied creditors to the new government."),
            ("The dinner-table compromise", "Jefferson hosted the meeting at which Madison accepted assumption while northern leaders accepted a permanent Potomac capital. This became the Compromise of 1790."),
            ("Bank and implied powers", "His defense of the First Bank argued that necessary means could be implied from enumerated powers. Washington accepted his reasoning over Jefferson's strict construction."),
            ("Manufactures, customs, and cutters", "His Report on Manufactures promoted industrial development, while his department created customs administration and the Revenue Cutter Service, an ancestor of the Coast Guard."),
            ("Whiskey tax and rebellion", "His excise on distilled spirits fell heavily on western producers and helped provoke the Whiskey Rebellion. He accompanied Washington's militia mobilization in 1794, demonstrating the enforcement power of the financial state he had designed."),
            ("Paterson and the useful-manufactures society", "He helped found the Society for Establishing Useful Manufactures, which planned the industrial city of Paterson around the Passaic Falls. Treasury aide Tench Coxe contributed research and drafting to his reports before later changing parties."),
